+### iPhone Duo portrait: game above the controls
+
+**This is only for the iPhone Duo's inner screen, and the setting is only shown on an iPhone Duo.** Every other iPhone and every iPad does not list it, stays in landscape and is not affected. (The Duo is recognized by its inner screen's shape; a Duo that has run a game on its inner screen is remembered, so the setting shows with the closed screen too.) When the iPhone Duo's inner screen is held upright, with this on (the default), the screen is split in half. The game's picture is centred in the top half, with whatever **Bars around the picture** says around it (a blurred copy of the picture, the still image, or black). The on-screen controls are all in the bottom half, over a dark background image (`dm/ios/Sources/controls_background.jpg`): the shoulder keys, − and + and the layout button along the top of that half, the left stick and D-pad at the bottom left, the face keys at the bottom right. Touches on the picture still go to the game's touch screen, so a pointer or touch game is played on the top half. Turn it off to get the picture centred with the controls over the whole screen, as before. The app allows portrait only on the iPhone Duo's inner screen once the game is running (the screen is recognized by its shape); every other screen, iPads included, and the settings screen, stay landscape.
+
+Control positions and sizes made in the [editor](#edit-controls-at-start) are offsets from the built-in places in each layout, so they carry over to the portrait layout, where the controls sit in a smaller area; adjust them there if they do not fit. A landscape screen is not affected.
+
